The `popstate` event listener is added with `{once: true}` but is not cleaned up if the component unmounts before the event fires. This creates a memory leak where `onModalHide` remains in memory and could reference stale props/state.

**Suggested fix:**
Store the listener in a ref and clean it up in the component unmount effect:

```tsx
const popstateListenerRef = useRef<(() => void) | null>(null);

const hideModal = () => {
    if ((window.history.state as WindowState)?.shouldGoBack && shouldHandleNavigationBack) {
        // Wait for history.back() to complete before calling onModalHide to prevent navigation race conditions
        popstateListenerRef.current = () => {
            onModalHide();
            popstateListenerRef.current = null;
        };
        window.addEventListener('popstate', popstateListenerRef.current, {once: true});
        window.history.back();
    } else {
        onModalHide();
    }
};

useEffect(
    () => () => {
        window.removeEventListener('popstate', handlePopState);
        // Clean up the hideModal popstate listener if it exists
        if (popstateListenerRef.current) {
            window.removeEventListener('popstate', popstateListenerRef.current);
            popstateListenerRef.current = null;
        }
    },
    [handlePopState],
);
```

