The recursive call to `compute()` inside `computeFieldPart()` lacks protection against circular field references, which could cause an infinite loop and stack overflow.

While `hasCircularReferences()` exists in this file to detect circular references, it is not being used here to prevent infinite recursion when fields reference each other in a cycle (e.g., A references B, B references A).

**Fix**: Add a depth limit or visited set to prevent infinite recursion:

```typescript
function computeFieldPart(part: FormulaPart, context?: FormulaContext, visitedFields?: Set<string>): string {
    const fieldName = part.fieldPath.at(0)?.toLowerCase();

    if (!fieldName) {
        return part.definition;
    }

    // Prevent circular references
    const visited = visitedFields ?? new Set<string>();
    if (visited.has(fieldName)) {
        return part.definition; // Return original definition if circular reference detected
    }

    if (context?.fieldsByName?.[fieldName]) {
        const field = context.fieldsByName[fieldName];
        const parsedModel = parse(field.defaultValue);
        const hasFieldRefs = parsedModel.some((p) => p.type === FORMULA_PART_TYPES.FIELD);
        if (hasFieldRefs) {
            visited.add(fieldName);
            return compute(field.defaultValue, {...context, visitedFields: visited});
        }
        return field.value ?? field.defaultValue ?? '';
    }

    if (context?.fieldValues?.[fieldName]) {
        return context.fieldValues[fieldName];
    }

    return part.definition;
}
```

